/-!
# Verified code generation from Lir to the extended RV64 model

`Silmaril.RiscV.Backend.compile` translates a Lir program over physical
registers (for example the output of `Lir.Alloc.spill`) into `RiscV.Ext`
machine code, and `compile_correct` relates the two: for an accepted program
and a memory layout satisfying `Space.Valid`, every returned result, trap and
divergence of a Lir run is matched by the run of the `RiscV.Ext` machine from
the constructed loaded state (`loadState`), unless that run reaches the
stack-overflow stub. `compile_correct_bounded` removes the overflow alternative
when the Lir run's call depth is bounded by `D` and the stack holds `D + 1`
frames. `spill_compile_correct` starts from virtual registers.

| Module | Content |
| --- | --- |
| `Lir.Regs` | register conventions and `Assign` |
| `Lir.Exec` | memory layout (`Space`), machine invariant `Good`, straight-line execution |
| `Lir.Arith` | constants, moves, unary and binary operators |
| `Lir.Bits` | `clz`, `ctz`, `popcnt` (64- and 32-bit) as unrolled sums |
| `Lir.Mem` | byte-wise linear-memory loads and stores, stack words |
| `Lir.Emit` | layout, emission, code location lemmas |
| `Lir.Check` | compilation errors, `compile` |
| `Lir.Frame` | frames: save/restore, argument and result transfer |
| `Lir.Correct` | the matching relation and the per-transition simulation |
| `Lir.Loader` | the loaded state and the end-to-end theorems |
| `Lir.Examples` | concrete compilations and one end-to-end instance |

## Scope

What is proved:

* forward preservation of returned values and final linear memory, traps with
  their memory, and divergence, from a Lir run to a run of the handwritten
  `RiscV.Ext` machine (`MState.step`), for every program `compile` accepts;
* the machine stops (`EBREAK`) at the halt, trap and overflow stubs, which have
  distinct addresses;
* `compile` rejects calls, parameters, results and returned values beyond
  `a0 … a7`, `JAL` offsets beyond ±1 MiB and frames beyond 12-bit offsets.
  Every Lir operator is selected.

What is not proved or not covered:

* stack exhaustion remains an outcome of `compile_correct`; it is excluded only
  by `compile_correct_bounded`, whose call-depth bound `D` is a hypothesis.
  Nothing computes `D` for a program, except `depthBounded_of_no_calls` for
  call-free programs, and the bounded theorem is not composed with spilling;
* nothing is claimed for Lir executions that get stuck (undefined behaviour);
  no backward simulation or machine safety theorem is stated;
* `clz`, `ctz`, `popcnt` and their 32-bit forms use about 3 instructions per
  bit (no Zbb in the `RiscV.Ext` model);
* the machine is the local handwritten `RiscV.Ext` model. Only the clauses
  listed in the Sail correspondence module (not in this repository: it needs the `riscv-zkvm` package) are related to the generated Sail model;
  loads, stores, `JALR`, the word forms and `EBREAK` are not. The generated code
  uses those instructions, so the end-to-end result holds for the local model,
  not for Sail or hardware;
* linear-memory accesses are byte-wise, so misaligned Lir accesses never meet
  the model's misaligned-access trap; the code is correct but not fast;
* a call saves and restores all 14 allocatable registers; there is no liveness
  information.
